'''bride-cup†''' (brīd′kup), ''n.'' A bowl or cup of spiced wine and other ingredients formerly served with bride-cake at wedding-feasts. Also called ''bride-bowl.''
{{smaller block|<poem>Get our bed ready, chamberlain;
Host, a ''bride-cup''; you have rare conceits,
And good ingredients. ''B. Jonson'', New Inn, v. 1.</poem>}}

'''bride-day''' (brīd′dā), ''n.'' The marriage-day. ''Scott.''

'''bridegroom''' (brīd′gröm), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. ''bridegrome'' (Tyndale, {{sc|a. d.}} 1525), with inserted ''r'' as in the simple ''groom'' (q. v.); < ME. ''bridegome, bridgume, bredgome, brudgume,'' < AS. ''brȳdguma,'' also ''brȳdiguma'' (''brȳdi'' for ''brȳde,'' gen. of ''brȳd'') (= OS. ''brūdigumo'' = OFries. ''breidgoma'' = D. ''bruidegom, bruigom'' = MLG. ''brūdegam,'' LG. ''brüdegam, brödegam, brögam'' = OHG. ''brūtigomo,'' MHG. ''briutegome,'' G. ''bräutigam'' = Icel. ''brudhgumi'' = Sw. ''brudgum, -gumme,'' = ODan. ''brudegomme, brudgomme,'' Dan. ''brudgom''), lit. bride’s man, < ''brȳd,'' gen. ''brȳde,'' etc., bride, + ''guma,'' man: see ''bride''<sup>1</sup> and ''groom.'' Cf. Goth. ''brūthfaths,'' bridegroom, < ''brūths,'' daughter-in-law (bride), + ''faths'' = Gr. πόσις = Skt. ''pati,'' husband, lord: see ''despot, potent,'' etc.] '''1.''' A man newly married, or about to be married.
{{smaller block|He that hath the bride is the ''bridegroom.'' John iii. 29.
<poem>Those dulcet sounds in break of day
That creep into the dreaming ''bridegroom’s'' ear,
And summon him to marriage. ''Shak.'', M. of V., iii. 2.</poem>}}
